[{:db/id 17592186339006 :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $CD=DC={I}_{3}$\n\nII. $CE=EC={I}_{3}$\n\nIII. $D={C}^{-1}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186410423 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Given the matrices above, write $A{x}^{T}⊙(y+{x}^{T})$ as a matrix product. \n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$A{x}^{T}⊙(y+{x}^{T})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-2}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"7px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-3}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n$0$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n$5$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n$4$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287712 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244115 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nII. $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nIII. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186410436 :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,3).$ \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120471 :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,…$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=18,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=2f(n)+3,\\,f(1)=1,\\,n\\ge 1$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233427 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\nII. There are two players aged $21.$\n\nIII. None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186275221 :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,1).$ \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
